editorial:
## Method and reasoning
XOR cancels pairs, leaving the XOR of the two singletons. Isolate a set bit with unsigned arithmetic to avoid signed overflow on the sign bit. The singletons differ on that bit, and each pair stays together in one group, so XOR within each group recovers its singleton. Compare the recovered values as signed integers.

## Walk through the sample
The 4s and 8s cancel, leaving −1 XOR 0. Any set bit of that XOR separates the two singletons. Each duplicated value goes wholly into one bucket, so both copies cancel there as well.

## Complexity
O(n) time and O(1) auxiliary space beyond the stored input.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);
    int n;
    cin >> n;
    vector<int64_t> a(n);
    uint64_t both = 0;
    for (auto &x : a) {
        cin >> x;
        both ^= uint64_t(x);
    }
    uint64_t bit = both & -both, l = 0, r = 0;
    for (int64_t x : a)
        (uint64_t(x) & bit ? l : r) ^= uint64_t(x);
    int64_t x = bit_cast<int64_t>(l), y = bit_cast<int64_t>(r);
    cout << min(x, y) << ' ' << max(x, y) << '\n';
}
~~~
